The di-jet invariant mass is \(M=\sqrt{(P_{3}+P_{4})^{2}}\), where \(P_{3}\) and \(P_{4}\) are the 4-momenta of two outgoing partons. We can express the individual 4-momenta as:

\[P_{3}=\begin{bmatrix}m_{T}\cosh y_{3}\\ p_{T}\cos\phi_{3}\\ p_{T,3}\sin\phi_{3}\\ m_{T,3}\sinh y_{3}\end{bmatrix},P_{4}=\begin{bmatrix}m_{T}\cosh y_{4}\\ p_{T}\cos\phi_{4}\\ p_{T,4}\sin\phi_{4}\\ m_{T,4}\sinh y_{4}\end{bmatrix}\] (B.3)

\[M=\sqrt{(P_{3}^{2}+P_{4}^{2}+2P_{3}*P_{4})^{2}},\] (B.4)

where \(P^{2}=m^{2}\), and the cross term is:

\[P_{3}*P_{4} =m_{T,3}m_{T,4}(\cosh y_{3}\cosh y_{4}-\sinh y_{3}\sinh y_{4})-p_ {T,3}p_{T,4}(\cos\phi_{3}\cos\phi_{4}+\sin\phi_{3}\sin\phi_{4})\] \[=\sqrt{m_{3}^{2}+p_{T3}^{2}}\sqrt{m_{4}^{2}+p_{T4}^{2}}cosh(y_{3} -y_{4})-2p_{T3}p_{T4}cos(\phi_{3}-\phi_{4}).\]

Then the di-jet invariant mass is:

\[M=\sqrt{m_{3}^{2}+m_{4}^{2}+2\sqrt{m_{3}^{2}+p_{T3}^{2}}\sqrt{m_{4}^{2}+p_{T4 }^{2}}cosh(y_{3}-y_{4})-2p_{T3}p_{T4}cos(\phi_{3}-\phi_{4})}.\] (B.6)

#### b.1.2 Scattering Kinematic Approximations

For simplicity, we assume that partons are massless and only move in the z-direction. Then the initial 4-momenta can be written as:

\[P_{1}=\frac{\sqrt{s}}{2}\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}\\ 0\\ 0\\ 0\\ x_{1}\end{bmatrix},\qquad P_{2}=\frac{\sqrt{s}}{2}\begin{bmatrix}x_{2}\\ 0\\ 0\\ x_{2}\end{bmatrix}.\] (B.7)Define the initial state 4-momentum as \(q=P_{1}+P_{2}\), the invariant M would be:

\[M=\sqrt{q^{2}}=\sqrt{sx_{1}x_{2}}.\] (B.8)

The pesudorapidity can be defined using the momentum fractions:

\[\cosh y =\frac{E}{\sqrt{E^{2}-p_{z}^{2}}}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{ \sqrt{x_{1}x_{2}}}\] (B.9a) \[\sinh y =\frac{p_{z}}{\sqrt{E^{2}-p_{z}^{2}}}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{x_{1}-x_{2 }}{\sqrt{x_{1}x_{2}}}.\] (B.9b)

Then the pesudorapidity of the momentum \(q\) is:

\[y=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac{E-p_{z}}{E+p_{z}}\right)=\frac{1}{2}\ln\left(\frac {x_{1}}{x_{2}}\right).\] (B.10)

The Lorentz transformation is given by:

\[\Lambda(y)=\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y&0&0\sinh y\\ 0&1&0&0\\ 0&0&1&0\\ \sinh y&0&0\cosh y\end{bmatrix}=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x_{1}x_{2}}}\begin{bmatrix}x _{1}+x_{2}&0&0&x_{1}-x_{2}\\ 0&1&0&0\\ 0&0&1&0\\ x_{1}-x_{2}&0&0&x_{1}+x_{2}\end{bmatrix}.\] (B.11)

In the center of mass frame, the outgoing partons can be written as:

\[P_{3}^{*}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y^{*}\\ \cos\phi\\ \sin\phi\\ \sinh y^{*}\end{bmatrix},\quad P_{4}^{*}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y^{*}\\ -\cos\phi\\ -\sin\phi\\ -\sinh y^{*}\end{bmatrix}.\] (B.12)

In the lab frame, using the Lorentz Transformation, we get:\[P_{3}=\Lambda(y)P_{3}^{*}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y&0&0&\sinh y\\ 0&1&0&0\\ 0&0&1&0\\ \sinh y&0&0&\cosh y\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y^{*}\\ \cos\phi\\ \sin\phi\\ \sinh y^{*}\end{bmatrix}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh(y+y^{*})\\ \cos\phi\\ \sin\phi\\ \sinh(y+y^{*})\end{bmatrix}\] (B.13) \[P_{4}=\Lambda(y)P_{4}^{*}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y&0&0&\sinh y \\ 0&1&0&0\\ 0&0&1&0\\ \sinh y&0&0&\cosh y\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y^{*}\\ -\cos\phi\\ -\sin\phi\\ -\sinh y^{*}\end{bmatrix}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh(y-y^{*})\\ -\cos\phi\\ -\sin\phi\\ \sinh(y-y^{*})\end{bmatrix}.\]

In the lab frame, we can define the outgoing partons as:

\[P_{3}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y_{3}\\ \cos\phi_{3}\\ \sin\phi_{3}\\ \sinh y_{3}\end{bmatrix},\quad P_{4}=p_{T}\begin{bmatrix}\cosh y_{4}\\ \cos\phi_{4}\\ \sin\phi_{4}\\ \sinh y_{4}\end{bmatrix}.\] (B.14)

Then \(y_{3}=y+y^{*}\) and \(y_{4}=y-y^{*}\), where \(y=\frac{y_{3}+y_{4}}{2}\) and \(y^{*}=\frac{y_{3}-y_{4}}{2}\). The invariant mass is \(M=2p_{T}\cosh y^{*}\) using the center-of-mass frame \(P_{3}^{*}+P_{4}^{*}\). Using equation B.10, we can rewrite the expression:

\[\begin{split} x_{1}&=\sqrt{x_{1}x_{2}}e^{y}=\frac{M}{ \sqrt{s}}e^{y}=\frac{p_{T}}{\sqrt{s}}2\cosh y^{*}e^{y}=\frac{p_{T}}{\sqrt{s}}( e^{y_{3}}+e^{y_{4}})\\ x_{2}&=\sqrt{x_{1}x_{2}}e^{-y}=\frac{p_{T}}{\sqrt{s}}(e^{-y_{3}}+e^{-y_{ 4}}).\end{split}\] (B.15)

The center-of-mass frame scattering angle can be expressed in terms of the pseudorapidity:

\[\cos\theta^{*}=\frac{\sinh y^{*}}{\sqrt{\cos^{2}\phi+\sin^{2}\phi+\sinh^{2}y^{ *}}}=\tanh y^{*}=\tanh\left(\frac{y_{3}-y_{4}}{2}\right).\] (B.16)

### \(A_{ll}\) Derivation

In this section, details of the derivation of expressions for \(A_{LL}\) and \(\delta A_{LL}\) will be shown. This calculation is based on Brian Page's thesis.

#### b.2.1 Double Spin Asymmetry, \(A_{ll}\)

The final \(A_{LL}\) values are the combination of different measurements from each run. For a single fill, \(A_{LL}\) can be expressed as:

\[A_{LL}=\frac{1}{P_{B}P_{Y}}\frac{(N^{++}+N^{--})-R(N^{+-}+N^{-+})}{(N^{++}+N^{ --})+R(N^{+-}+N^{-+})},\] (B.17)

where \(P_{B}\) and \(P_{Y}\) are the polarizations of the beams, and R is the relative luminosity, while the \(N^{++}\) and \(N^{+-}\) are the spin-sorted yields. To simply the calculation, we can use the single-spin asymmetry with fewer terms:

\[A_{i}=\frac{1}{P_{i}}\frac{N_{i}^{+}-R_{i}N_{i}^{-}}{N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-}}.\] (B.18)

The associated uncertainty for \(A_{i}\) can then be expressed as:

\[\begin{split}\sigma_{i}^{2}&=(\frac{\partial A_{i }}{\partial N_{i}^{+}}\sigma_{N_{i}^{+}})^{2}+(\frac{\partial A_{i}}{\partial N _{i}^{-}}\sigma_{N_{i}^{-}})^{2}+(\frac{\partial A_{i}}{\partial P_{i}}\sigma _{P_{i}})^{2}+(\frac{\partial A_{i}}{\partial R_{i}}\sigma_{R_{i}})^{2}\\ &=\frac{1}{P_{i}^{2}(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-})^{2}}[(1-P_{i}A_{i})^{ 2}\sigma_{N_{i}^{+}}^{2}+(1+P_{i}A_{i})^{2}R_{i}^{2}\sigma_{N_{i}^{-}}^{2}+(1+ P_{i}A_{i})^{2}(N^{-})^{2}\sigma_{R_{i}}^{2}]+\frac{A_{i}^{2}}{P_{i}^{2}} \sigma_{P_{i}}^{2}.\end{split}\] (B.19)

To get the best value of the asymmetry (\(\bar{A}\)), the maximum likelihood method is used to minimize the function:

\[\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{A_{i}-\bar{A}}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}.\] (B.20)Then we can get:

\[2\chi\frac{\partial\chi}{\partial x_{i}} =\sum_{i=1}^{N}2(\frac{A_{i}-\bar{A}}{\sigma_{i}})(\frac{1}{\sigma _{i}})=0\] \[\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{A_{i}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}} =\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\bar{A}}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\] (B.21) \[\bar{A} =\frac{\sum_{i}^{N}(A_{i}/\sigma_{i}^{2})}{\sum_{i}^{N}(1/\sigma _{i}^{2})}.\]

To get the final expression, we need to calculate and simplify the \(\sigma_{i}\). The scale uncertainty from polarization is about 6.5%, \(A_{i}\sim 10^{-2}\), \(P_{i}\sim 50\%\), \((\sigma_{N_{i}})^{2}=N\), \(R_{i}\sim 1\) and \(\sigma_{R_{i}}\sim 0\). With these assumptions, we can ignore the last two terms, and the uncertainty can be approximated as:

\[\sigma_{i}^{2}\approx\frac{1}{P_{i}^{2}(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-})}.\] (B.22)

Then the \(\bar{A}\) is:

\[\bar{A}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}(N_{i}^{+}-R_{i}N^{-})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{2 }(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-})}.\] (B.23)

Replacing the polarization and spin state yields, the final double spin asymmetry \(A_{LL}\) can be expressed as:

\[A_{LL}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{Bi}P_{Y,i}[(N_{i}^{++}+N_{i}^{--})-R_{3,i}(N_{i}^ {+-}+N_{i}^{-+})]}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{Bi}^{2}P_{Y,i}^{2}[(N_{i}^{++}+N_{i}^{--})+ R_{3,i}(N_{i}^{+-}+N_{i}^{-+})]}.\] (B.24)

#### b.2.2 \(A_{ll}\) Uncertainty

Similar to what has been calculated before, the uncertainty in \(\bar{A}\) can be expressed as:

\[\sigma_{\bar{A}}^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}[(\frac{\partial\bar{A}}{\partial N_{i}^{+ }}\sigma_{N_{i}^{+}})^{2}+(\frac{\partial\bar{A}}{\partial N_{i}^{-}}\sigma_{ N_{i}^{-}})^{2}+(\frac{\partial\bar{A}}{\partial P_{i}}\sigma_{P_{i}})^{2}+( \frac{\partial\bar{A}}{\partial R_{i}}\sigma_{R_{i}})^{2}].\] (B.25)

We still ignore the last two terms here, and the differential can be written as:

\[\frac{\partial\bar{A}}{\partial N_{j}^{+}}=\frac{P_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{ 2}(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-})}(1-P_{j}\bar{A})\] (B.26)\[\frac{\partial\tilde{A}}{\partial N_{j}^{-}}=\frac{-P_{j}R_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i }^{2}(N_{i}^{-}+R_{i}N^{-})}(1+P_{j}\tilde{A}).\] (B.27)

Then the uncertainty is:

\[\sigma_{\tilde{A}}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{2}[(1-P_{i}\tilde{A})^{2}N_{i }^{+}+(1+P_{i}\tilde{A})^{2}R_{i}^{2}N_{i}^{-}]}{(\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{2}(N_{i }^{+}+R_{i}N^{-}))^{2}}\] (B.28)

. \(\tilde{A}\sim 10^{-2}\) is small and can be neglected in the expression. Then the final expression is:

\[\sigma_{\tilde{A}}^{2}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{2}(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}^{2}N_{i}^ {-})}{[\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{i}^{2}(N_{i}^{+}+R_{i}N^{-})]^{2}}.\] (B.29)

Placing the proper beam polarization, relative luminosity, and yield factors into the equation, then the statistical uncertainty of \(A_{LL}\) is:

\[\delta A_{LL}=\frac{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{B,i}^{2}P_{Y,i}^{2}[(N_{i}^{++}+N_{ i}^{--})+R_{3,i}^{2}(N_{i}^{+-}+N_{i}^{-+})]}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}P_{B,i}^{2}P_{Y,i}^{2}[(N_{ i}^{++}+N_{i}^{--})+R_{3,i}(N_{i}^{+-}+N_{i}^{-+})]}.\] (B.30)

The statistical error is approximately equal to \(\delta A_{LL}\approx\frac{1}{P_{B}P_{Y}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\), where \(P_{Y,B}\) are the average beam polarizations for blue and yellow beams, and N is the total yield, summed over all spin states.

### \(\chi^{2}\) Derivation

In this section I will show the derivation of the \(\chi^{2}\) analysis. The calculation is based on [86] appendix B. The purpose of this calculation is to derive the appropriate expression of the \(\chi^{2}\) for data with correlated systematic errors. The defining condition is that \(\chi^{2}\) should obey a chi-squared distribution, and it is:

\[\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i}-\sum_{j}\beta_{ji}r_{j}}{\sigma_{i}} )^{2}+\sum_{j=1}^{K}r_{j}^{2},\] (B.31)

where \(\{m_{i}\}\) is a set of measurements, and \(i=1,2,3,...,N\). \(t_{i}\) is the true value (theoretical value of the \(i\)th measured quantity). \(\sigma_{i}\) is the uncorrelated error of measurement. And there are K correlated errors denoted as \(\beta_{1i}\), \(\beta_{2i}\),..., \(\beta_{Ki}\).

\(\chi^{2}\) is a function of \(r_{1},...,r_{K}\), and these variables could be used as fitting parameters to account for the systematic errors: minimizing \(\chi^{2}\) with respect to \(r_{j}\) can provide the best model to correct for the systematic error.

We can rewrite the expression to be:

\[\chi^{2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i}}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}-\sum_{j=1}^{K}2B_{ j}r_{j}+\sum_{j,j^{\prime}=1}^{K}A_{jj^{\prime}}r_{j}r_{j^{\prime}},\] (B.32)

where \(B_{j}\) is a vector with K components or (\(K\times 1\) matrix),

\[B\to B_{j}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\beta_{ji}(m_{i}-t_{i})/\sigma_{i}^{2},\] (B.33)

and \(A_{jj^{\prime}}\) is a \(K\times K\) symmetric matrix, is:

\[A\to A_{jj^{\prime}}=\delta_{jj^{\prime}}+\sum_{i=1}^{N}\beta_{ji}\beta_{j^{ \prime}i}/\sigma_{i}^{2}.\] (B.34)

If we define the free parameters \(r_{j}\) as a \(K\times 1\) matrix, then to minimize the \(\chi^{2}\) we get:

\[2\chi\frac{\partial\chi}{\partial r_{j^{\prime}}} =-2B_{j^{\prime}}+2\sum_{j=1}^{K}A_{jj^{\prime}}r_{j}=0\] \[\sum_{j=1}^{K}A_{jj^{\prime}}r_{j} =B_{j^{\prime}}\] (B.35) \[Ar =B\] \[r =A^{-1}B.\]Then the final expression of the \(\chi^{2}\) is:

\[\begin{split}\chi^{2}&=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i }}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}-2B^{T}r+r^{T}Ar\\ &=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i}}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}-2B^{T}A^{-1}B +(A^{-1}B)^{T}AA^{-1}B\\ &=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i}}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}-B^{T}A^{-1}B \\ &=\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\frac{m_{i}-t_{i}}{\sigma_{i}})^{2}-\sum_{j}^{K} \sum_{j^{\prime}}^{K}B_{j}(A^{-1})_{jj^{\prime}}B_{j^{\prime}}.\end{split}\] (B.36)

## Appendix C Code Structure and Parameters

### EEMC Gains Into the Simulation

For the 2009 pp 200 GeV data, the EEMC calibration was done much later than the embedding production, which means the EEMC trigger information may not be very precise in the simulation. A significant amount of computing time is needed to fully simulate the STAR detector response to a Pythia event, so an afterburner method was needed for the measurements in the EEMC region.

In order to implement the method, we need to figure out the program flow of the simulation. Simulation starts from the Pythia events (physics), which then go into Geant. The simulated energies are multiplied by the EMC tower gain factors from the database to get the corresponding ADC values, and then converted to the same format as the data. The ADC values are used in the trigger simulator for the trigger category determination.

The idea is to recalculate the skim trees, which store all the trigger informations from the jet finder. The method is simple: change the tower adc to a new value by \(Adc_{new}=Adc_{old}\times\frac{Gain_{new}}{Gain_{old}}+pedestal\) before the event goes into the trigger simulator. The key simulators for EEMC are the L2Emulator and EemcTriggerSimu. As shown in chapter 2, the data and simulation agreement is better after we implemented the new EEMC gains.

